A||||2||merlin||Replacing Cat without George Foreman||||5||
Z||000000||merlin||01-05-2001||08:40 AM||||Can the Catalytic Converter be replaced on a V6 without bringing it to a place like Meineke.  It looks like the one that's on there now is welded into place.  Is there a kit that allows installation without welding?  In addition, what's a good Cat for the V6? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||01-05-2001||09:19 AM||||The Fiero Store sells cat kits for all Fieros for around $120.  What you get is a long J-shaped piece of exhaust pipe and a new cat which is much smaller and lighter than the original.<P>To install it, you cut the old cat off at the downstream end and unbolt it from the head pipe at the upstream end.  The Fiero Store's J-pipe is so long your could install it and run without a cat - or you can cut a section out that's shorter than the cat and bolt the cat on, which I did.<P>It would have worked great if the head pipe-to-exhaust pipe bolts hadn't broken off, so I had to drill them out, retap the holes, and install new bolts.  Then I cut too much off the J-pipe and had to hillbilly-rig a patch from my leftover pipe.  The patch leaked, so I ended up going to Tuffy and paying to have the whole thing welded anyway.<P>Exhaust work sucks.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||01-05-2001||09:24 AM||||Rodney Dickman also sells a clamp-on cat.  Hacksaw your old one off; clamp this one on.  OK, it's not quite that easy, but it's not too tough as exhaust work goes.<P>Exhaust work <B>does</B> suck!<BR> <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com</A> <BR>||||reg||1
